```{r setup, include=FALSE}
library(wsMed)
options(width = 80)
knitr::opts_chunk$set(
  collapse = TRUE,
  comment = "#>",
  cache = FALSE,
  fig.width = 7,
  fig.height = 4.5
)
```

Use the [Get started examples](GetStarted.html) for a first analysis. This guide
shows which arguments to add when your question needs a different model,
covariates, missing-data handling or inference method.

## Start with the defaults

```{r quick-start}
data(example_data)
result <- wsMed(
  data = example_data,
  M_C1 = c("A1", "B1"),
  M_C2 = c("A2", "B2"),
  Y_C1 = "D1",
  Y_C2 = "D2"
)
print(result)
```

The default is parallel mediation with Monte Carlo inference, 20,000 draws,
95% CIs and unstandardized output. Listwise deletion is the default missing-data
strategy; the example data are complete. Later calls keep these defaults unless
an argument is explicitly changed.

## Paired variables

Each row represents one participant. Match each mediator's condition 1 and
condition 2 measurements by position in `M_C1` and `M_C2`. `Y_C1` and `Y_C2`
identify the outcome pair. Differences are always **condition 2 minus condition 1**.

| Construct | Condition 1 | Condition 2 | Role |
|---|---|---|---|
| A | `A1` | `A2` | First mediator (`M1`) |
| B | `B1` | `B2` | Second mediator (`M2`) |
| D | `D1` | `D2` | Outcome (`Y`) |

Outcomes, mediators and within-subject covariates are analyzed as continuous
numeric variables. Binary and multicategory variables can be between-subject
covariates or moderators; use unordered factors with explicit levels.
See [categorical predictors](CategoricalPredictors.html).

## Choose the mediator structure

### Predefined models

| `form` | Structure |
|---|---|
| `"P"` (default) | Parallel mediation |
| `"CN"` | Serial mediation; each mediator predicts every later mediator |
| `"CP"` | Serial-parallel mediation; M1 predicts the remaining mediators |
| `"PC"` | Parallel-serial mediation; the remaining mediators predict M1 |

The diagrams show the four predefined structures. Each includes condition
effects on every mediator and the outcome, and paths from every mediator
to the outcome. The connections among mediators distinguish the models.

<div class="wsmed-plot-grid" style="grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 300px), 1fr));">
<div class="wsmed-plot-card">
<p><strong>P: Parallel</strong></p>
<img src="Wa.png" alt="P model: parallel mediator pathways" width="100%">
<p>No directed connections among mediators. <a href="GenerateModelP.html">Model equations</a>.</p>
</div>
<div class="wsmed-plot-card">
<p><strong>CN: Serial</strong></p>
<img src="Wb.png" alt="CN model: earlier mediators predict later mediators" width="100%">
<p>Each earlier mediator predicts every later mediator. <a href="GenerateModelCN.html">Model equations</a>.</p>
</div>
<div class="wsmed-plot-card">
<p><strong>CP: Serial-parallel</strong></p>
<img src="Wc.png" alt="CP model: M1 predicts the remaining parallel mediators" width="100%">
<p>M1 predicts M2, M3, and so on. <a href="GenerateModelCP.html">Model equations</a>.</p>
</div>
<div class="wsmed-plot-card">
<p><strong>PC: Parallel-serial</strong></p>
<img src="Wd.png" alt="PC model: the parallel mediators predict M1" width="100%">
<p>M2, M3, and so on predict M1. <a href="GenerateModelPC.html">Model equations</a>.</p>
</div>
</div>

Click a diagram to enlarge it. In these difference-score diagrams, `diff`
denotes condition 2 minus condition 1 and `avg` denotes the centered average;
the triangle represents the constant used to estimate condition effects.

For example, change only `form` to fit a serial model:

```{r serial-model, eval=FALSE}
serial <- wsMed(
  data = example_data,
  M_C1 = c("A1", "B1"),
  M_C2 = c("A2", "B2"),
  Y_C1 = "D1",
  Y_C2 = "D2",
  form = "CN"
)
print(serial)
```

Choose mediator order from the research design. The model articles show the
[parallel](GenerateModelP.html), [serial](GenerateModelCN.html),
[serial-parallel](GenerateModelCP.html) and [parallel-serial](GenerateModelPC.html)
equations and diagrams.

### User-defined models

Use `form = "UD"` when you want to specify the mediator connections yourself.
Mediator labels follow the order in `M_C1` and `M_C2`: in the call below,
`M1` is A, `M2` is B, and `M3` is C. `Y` is the outcome pair D1/D2.
In `paths`, write one arrow per string using these labels:

```{r custom-paths}
custom_paths <- c(
  "M1 -> M3",  # A predicts C
  "M3 -> Y",   # C predicts the outcome
  "M2 -> Y"    # B predicts the outcome
)
```

Pass this vector to `wsMed()`; all other settings keep their defaults:

```{r custom-model}
custom <- wsMed(
  data = example_data,
  M_C1 = c("A1", "B1", "C1"),
  M_C2 = c("A2", "B2", "C2"),
  Y_C1 = "D1",
  Y_C2 = "D2",
  form = "UD",
  paths = custom_paths
)
print(custom)
```

Only the specified mediator connections are fitted. For example, `M1 -> Y`
is absent here; add that string to `custom_paths` if the model should include
it. Paths from the condition to every mediator and the direct condition-to-outcome
path are added automatically, so do not include `X` in `paths`.

The arrows must not form a cycle, and at least one must end at `Y`.
The [worked UD example](GenerateModelCustom.html) explains these rules and
shows how the specified arrows determine the indirect effects.

To add moderation, keep `custom_paths` and add `W = "D3"` and
`MP = c("b_1_3", "b3")`. This selects the M1-to-M3 and M3-to-outcome slopes
for moderation by D3. Unlike `paths`, `MP` takes coefficient labels, not
arrow strings. See the [UD moderation example](GenerateModelCustom.html#add-a-moderator)
for the full call, path-label choices, and conditional results.

## Add optional model components

### Covariates

Use `C_C1` and `C_C2` for paired within-subject covariates and `C` for
between-subject covariates. Their measurements remain in the same wide dataset:

```{r covariates, eval=FALSE}
adjusted <- wsMed(
  data = example_data,
  M_C1 = c("A1", "B1"),
  M_C2 = c("A2", "B2"),
  Y_C1 = "D1",
  Y_C2 = "D2",
  C_C1 = "C1",
  C_C2 = "C2",
  C = "D3"
)
print(adjusted)
```

### Moderators

Use `W` for the moderator column and `MP` for the paths to moderate. In the
parallel model, `b1` and `b2` are the mediator-to-outcome difference slopes:

```{r moderator, eval=FALSE}
moderated <- wsMed(
  data = example_data,
  M_C1 = c("A1", "B1"),
  M_C2 = c("A2", "B2"),
  Y_C1 = "D1",
  Y_C2 = "D2",
  W = "D3",
  MP = c("b1", "b2")
)
print(moderated)
plot(moderated)
```

Moderator main effects enter the difference equations automatically. For a
continuous moderator, the default report uses the centering reference, whereas
the default plot shows curves over the observed range. These two displays answer
different questions. The second [Get started example](GetStarted.html#example-2-add-a-continuous-moderator)
shows both outputs. Use `at` only to request particular raw moderator values.

For a categorical moderator, declare an unordered factor, choose its reference
level, and pass its column name to `W`. See the
[categorical example](CategoricalPredictors.html#fit-categorical-moderated-mediation).

## Missing data and confidence intervals

### Choose a missing-data strategy

| `Na` | Data handling | Available CI methods |
|---|---|---|
| `"DE"` (default) | Listwise deletion | Monte Carlo or bootstrap |
| `"FIML"` | Full-information maximum likelihood | Monte Carlo or bootstrap |
| `"MI"` | Multiple imputation | Monte Carlo |

To illustrate these choices, remove a few simulated post-condition measurements:

```{r missing-data}
incomplete <- example_data
incomplete$A2[1:5] <- NA
incomplete$D2[6:10] <- NA
```

Add `Na = "FIML"` when you want FIML:

```{r fiml, eval=FALSE}
fiml_result <- wsMed(
  data = incomplete,
  M_C1 = c("A1", "B1"),
  M_C2 = c("A2", "B2"),
  Y_C1 = "D1",
  Y_C2 = "D2",
  Na = "FIML"
)
print(fiml_result)
```

Use `Na = "MI"` for multiple imputation. The default uses five predictive
mean matching imputations for the numeric variables in this example:

```{r mi, eval=FALSE}
mi_result <- wsMed(
  data = incomplete,
  M_C1 = c("A1", "B1"),
  M_C2 = c("A2", "B2"),
  Y_C1 = "D1",
  Y_C2 = "D2",
  Na = "MI"
)
print(mi_result)
```

For finer control, `mi_args` accepts imputation settings; for example,
`mi_args = list(m = 20)` requests 20 imputations. Select the imputation method
and number of imputations for your data and analysis.

MI imputes the original measurements before constructing analysis variables
within each completed dataset. FIML handles missing analysis variables during
fitting, after differences, averages and interactions have been constructed.
FIML requires categorical predictors to be fully observed.

Choose a strategy that is appropriate for the missing-data assumptions and
variable roles. With moderation, default main-effects imputation may not preserve
the substantive interactions; see [model-compatible imputation](CompatibleImputation.html).

### Choose the inference method

Monte Carlo is the default. To combine FIML with bootstrap CIs, specify
both `Na` and `ci_method`. This executed example uses the incomplete data above
and the default 2,000 bootstrap replicates:

```{r bootstrap}
boot_result <- wsMed(
  data = incomplete,
  M_C1 = c("A1", "B1"),
  M_C2 = c("A2", "B2"),
  Y_C1 = "D1",
  Y_C2 = "D2",
  Na = "FIML",
  ci_method = "bootstrap"
)
print(boot_result)
```

When one member of a measurement pair is missing, its difference and average
are missing too. FIML handles these constructed variables during fitting; the
observed member of the original pair is not separately included in this model.
The bootstrap resamples participant rows of the constructed analysis data,
retaining the within-participant pairing and fitting each sample with FIML.
For complete data or listwise deletion, omit `Na = "FIML"` from this call.

`R` controls Monte Carlo draws,
`bootstrap` controls bootstrap replicates, and `alpha` controls the confidence
level as `1 - alpha`. `seed` and `iseed` control the Monte Carlo and bootstrap
random draws, respectively. `ci_method = "both"` retains both inference engines;
`print()` then reports them separately. MI currently supports Monte Carlo only.

## Standardized effects

The default output is unstandardized. Add `standardized = TRUE` to the analysis
to save standardized reporting as its default, or request it for one summary:

```{r standardized-results}
summary(
  result,
  standardized = TRUE
)
```

Changing the reporting scale does not fit a new model. Standardized CIs propagate
uncertainty in the coefficients and model-implied scales; they are not obtained
by dividing the unstandardized CI endpoints by a single fitted SD.
See [standardized effects](StandardizedModeratedMediation.html).

## Inspect and present the results

```{r results}
summary(result)
plot(result)
```

Use `print(result)` for the core report and `summary(result)` for a structured
statistical summary. Their default displays can be read directly. Numerical
extraction, selected moderator values and export are optional next steps in
[Read and export results](ResultsGuide.html).

For generated model syntax, use `printGM(result)`. For separate model
specification, fitting, inspection and inference, use the
[staged workflow](ModularWorkflow.html).

## Manuscript data

The package also includes the empirical dataset used in the paper:

```{r manuscript-data}
data(wsmed_examples)
dim(wsmed_examples)
```

It contains 123 intervention-group participants and retains the original missing
values. Its help page documents the study, processing, variable definitions and
license. The examples on this page use simulated data to teach analysis choices;
the paper's replication materials record the full settings and computing
environment for its numerical results.
